{{also|grease-monkey}}
==English==

===Etymology===
First use appears c. 1910 in a translated work. See cite below. May have originated during the [[w:Industrial Revolution|Industrial Revolution]] in Great Britain, when children were used to grease the [[w:line shaft|large rotating axles]] which were used to transfer power from one centralized steam engine to all of the machines on the factory floor. These children, covered in grease and crawling in the tight spaces in the ceilings, were equated with monkeys.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-grease monkey.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|slang}} A [[mechanic]], often with the specific connotation of an automobile mechanic.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|title=Jeanne d'Arc et l'Union française  - discours prononcé à Notre-Dame le 8 Mai 1910 en la première fête liturgique de la Bienheureuse Jeanne d'Arc|author=Antonin Gilbert Sertillanges |page=10|passage=If you appear like a tramp or '''grease-monkey''' how can you expect to meet the people who are able to support your business.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|title=Motor World for Jobbers, Dealers and Garagemen - Volume 55|author=Motor World Publishing Company (publisher)|page=29|passage=Maybe some other "'''grease monkey'''," as I call mechanics, will correct me.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|title=The Elevator Constructor - Volume 17|author=International Union of Elevator Constructors (publisher)|page=30|passage=Brother W.M. Hillery resigned from his job as '''grease monkey''' and is working for Armour and Company, but as we understand he is just itching to get with the old bunch.}}

